::MCR_U03_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
Which display best compares favorite lunch choices: pizza, salad, sandwich, or pasta?
{
=A. bar graph#Correct. A bar graph compares category frequencies clearly.
~B. histogram#A histogram is for numerical intervals, not named categories.
~C. line graph#A line graph is best for change over time, not category comparison.
~D. scatterplot#A scatterplot compares two numerical variables, not one categorical variable.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A bar graph shows 22 students chose bus and 15 chose car. What conclusion is supported?
{
~A. Every student rides the bus.#The display compares two categories, but it does not say every student chose bus.
=B. More students chose bus than car.#Correct. The bar for bus is larger than the bar for car.
~C. Car is the most common choice.#Bus has the larger count, so car is not more common here.
~D. The survey measured student heights.#The categories are transportation choices, not numerical heights.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
A teacher records quiz scores from 0 to 100 for 80 students. Which display best shows the distribution of scores by intervals?
{
~A. bar graph#A bar graph is mainly for separate categories.
~B. two-way table#A two-way table organizes two categorical variables, not score intervals.
=C. histogram#Correct. A histogram groups numerical data into intervals.
~D. circle graph#A circle graph shows parts of a whole but not distribution shape as clearly.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A line graph of cafeteria sales rises from Monday to Friday. What does the upward trend mean?
{
~A. Sales stayed exactly the same.#An upward trend means the values increased.
~B. The graph compares favorite foods.#A line graph over weekdays shows change over time.
~C. Friday sales were zero.#An upward trend does not mean the final value is zero.
=D. Sales increased over the week.#Correct. A rising line over time shows an increase.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
Which data type is represented by shirt sizes small, medium, large, and extra large?
{
~A. numerical data#The values are labels, not measured numbers.
=B. categorical data#Correct. Shirt sizes are labels or categories.
~C. bivariate numerical data#There are not two numerical variables here.
~D. time-series data#The values are not recorded over time.
}
